time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\\frac{1}{10}(2)^{5}-\\frac{3}{4}(2)^{4}+\\frac{1}{2}(2)^{2}-10 \\\\  & =3.2-12+2-10 \\\\  & =-16.8\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $16.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139534611 true] [17592186298713 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298714 13194139534611 true] [17592186298713 1089 "q-102695" 13194139534611 true] [17592186298713 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534611 true] [17592186298713 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in $\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=2t+{t}^{2}$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. If the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}$, calculate the velocity of the particle at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}.$" 13194139534611 true] [17592186298713 1113 #uuid "7b207322-8b81-50ed-8b36-b439725ea8bf" 13194139534611 true] [17592186298713 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (2t+{t}^{2})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=\\frac{8}{3}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{3} & =(1)^{2}+\\frac{(1)^{3}}{3}+C \\\\ \\frac{8}{3} & =1+\\frac{1}{3}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{4}{3}+{t}^{2}+\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =\\frac{4}{3}+(2)^{2}+\\frac{(2)^{3}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}+4+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =8\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534611 true] [17592186298714 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298715 13194139534611 true] [17592186298714 1000 #edb/ref 17592186298716 13194139534611 true] [17592186298714 1000 #ed