] [17592186298728 1104 #edb/ref 17592186298729 13194139534611 true] [17592186298728 1110 #uuid "9db53bbf-d2a6-53a8-a7ea-d01da555949b" 13194139534611 true] [17592186298729 1034 "2\\sqrt{t+1}-\\sqrt{2}" 13194139534611 true] [17592186298729 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534611 true] [17592186298729 1111 #uuid "e940ff3e-9444-56f1-8766-f00d48882745" 13194139534611 true] [17592186298730 1017 #edb/ref 17592186298731 13194139534611 true] [17592186298730 1089 "q-351059" 13194139534611 true] [17592186298730 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534611 true] [17592186298730 1100 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+1}},$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{ft/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=3\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139534611 true] [17592186298730 1113 #uuid "e592dddf-ea83-5434-96c1-24b4d0792215" 13194139534611 true] [17592186298730 1145 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{t+1}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{t+1}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =6\\sqrt{0+1}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =6+C \\\\ C & =-\\frac{11}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=6\\sqrt{t+1}-\\frac{11}{2}.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=3\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(3) & =6\\sqrt{3+1}-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =6(2)-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{2}-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the 